---
import '../styles/global.css';

interface Props {
  title: string;
  description: string;
  // A square picture for link previews. App pages pass their icon; everything else
  // gets the mark. Square because a chat app crops a preview to its own shape, and a
  // phone screenshot cropped to a square is a strip of status bar.
  image?: string;
}
const { title, description, image = '/assets/mark-256.png' } = Astro.props;
// Absolute, because a preview is fetched by somebody else's server that has no idea
// which site the path belonged to.
const canonical = new URL(Astro.url.pathname, Astro.site).href;
const imageUrl = new URL(image, Astro.site).href;
const isNotFound = Astro.url.pathname.startsWith('/404');
const year = new Date().getFullYear();
// "Work" is a real page now, not an anchor. It was "/#work" back when the catalogue
// sat under the hero on the homepage — a link that, pressed from the homepage, scrolled
// a little and appeared to do nothing. The work moved to /apps/ and the link moved with
// it; there is nothing at /#work to scroll to any more.
const nav = [
  { href: '/apps/', label: 'Work' },
  { href: '/about/', label: 'About' },
];
---
<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    {/*
      Keep it first in <head>: a meta CSP governs only what the parser sees after it.

      ── What changed, and what did not ──
      This used to be `default-src 'none'` with NOTHING granting script-src, because the
      site shipped no script at all. It now ships two on every page — /motion.js and
      /globe.js — plus whichever field /globe.js decides the device can draw, so the
      policy is 'self' instead of nothing. The grant has not widened as files were
      added; 'self' covered them already, which is the point of the note below about a
      hash being the tighter policy.

      The thing that grant was protecting against is unchanged. Markdown in /apps is
      rendered with <Content />, and Astro passes raw HTML in a markdown body straight
      through to the page: a <script> tag written into an app file WOULD run. Built and
      checked, back when this was 'none', that it does. `script-src 'self'` still blocks
      the two shapes that matter — an inline <script> and anything loaded from another
      origin — so a bad app file can no more execute today than it could before. What it
      can now do is load a first-party path, which is the one hole 'self' opens; there is
      no upload path into this repo that does not go through a commit, so the cost was
      judged worth the two effects the script buys. If that ever stops being true, the
      tighter policy is a hash: 'sha256-…' for the one file.

      What each grant is for, so nothing gets widened by guesswork:
        script-src 'self'           /motion.js and /globe.js, plus whichever field
                                    /globe.js loads: /three.min.js and /globe-scene.js
                                    on a wide screen, or /sky-mobile.js everywhere else.
                                    Nothing else, and nothing from another origin:
                                    three.js is vendored into /public rather than pulled
                                    from a CDN, which is what keeps "no third-party
                                    scripts" true as a fact about the network and not
                                    just about intent.

                                    They are deliberately real files in /public rather
                                    than bundled /_astro hashes, because the about page,
                                    README.md and src/site.ts invite the reader to
                                    open their network panel and count the requests — so
                                    every script has to be findable and named.
        img-src   'self' data:      favicon, screenshots, and inline SVG data URIs
        style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'
                                    build.inlineStylesheets is 'always', and the curio
                                    deck, the device's crossfade keyframes and the
                                    showcase's tab rules all inject with is:inline — all
                                    inline styles, so this cannot be tightened without
                                    changing how the site is built
        font-src  'self'            the Bricolage Grotesque and Albert Sans woff2
                                    files, self-hosted
        base-uri / form-action 'none'
                                    there is no <base> and no <form> on the site; these
                                    stop an injected one from retargeting a link or
                                    posting somewhere

      There is still no connect-src. That is the grant that would let a script talk to a
      server, and its absence is what makes "this page is not watching you" enforced
      rather than promised: none of these scripts could phone home if it wanted to, and
      neither could anything that replaced them. That includes the vendored three.js —
      it is a rendering library with no network code, and the policy means it could not
      use one if it had it.

      Links out (tally.so, github.com, the curio sources) are navigations, which CSP does
      not govern — they keep working.

      What this canNOT do: frame-ancestors is ignored in a meta tag, and GitHub Pages
      cannot send real headers, so the site stays framable. See the security notes.
    */}
    <meta
      http-equiv="Content-Security-Policy"
      content="default-src 'none'; script-src 'self'; img-src 'self' data:; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; font-src 'self'; base-uri 'none'; form-action 'none'"
    />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
    <title>{title}</title>
    <meta name="description" content={description} />
    <meta property="og:title" content={title} />
    <meta property="og:description" content={description} />
    <meta property="og:type" content="website" />
    <meta property="og:image" content={imageUrl} />
    <meta name="twitter:card" content="summary" />
    {/* No canonical on the 404: it is served at every missing address, and a canonical
        would tell a search engine that all of them are one real page. */}
    {!isNotFound && <link rel="canonical" href={canonical} />}
    {!isNotFound && <meta property="og:url" content={canonical} />}
    {/* PNG rather than SVG, because the mark is artwork rather than a drawn glyph now.
        32px is what a browser tab uses; 180px covers bookmarks and an iOS home screen.
        Be aware the cube is not legible at 16px — see DESIGN.md § The wordmark. */}
    <link rel="icon" type="image/png" sizes="32x32" href="/favicon-32.png" />
    <link rel="icon" type="image/png" sizes="180x180" href="/favicon-180.png" />
    <link rel="apple-touch-icon" href="/favicon-180.png" />
    {/* Both faces are preloaded because both are above the fold on every page:
        Bricolage sets the headline, Albert Sans sets the line under it. Only the latin
        cuts are preloaded — the latin-ext files are fetched on demand, and preloading a
        file most visitors never need would cost them 54 KB for nothing. The mono role
        uses whatever the OS already has, so it costs nothing either way. */}
    <link rel="preload" href="/fonts/bricolage-grotesque-latin.woff2" as="font" type="font/woff2" crossorigin />
    <link rel="preload" href="/fonts/albert-sans-latin.woff2" as="font" type="font/woff2" crossorigin />

    {/* The first script. `is:inline` keeps Astro's hands off it: left to
        itself Astro would bundle it into /_astro/<hash>.js, and the copy on the about
        page and in src/site.ts tells the reader to count the requests in their
        network panel — a named file at a stable path is what makes that check easy
        rather than a puzzle.

        `defer` and not `async`: nothing here is urgent, and it must not compete with
        the fonts, which are what the reader is actually waiting for. Everything it does
        has a correct CSS resting state, so it arriving late — or never — costs nothing.
        See public/motion.js for the rules it has to keep. */}
    <script is:inline src="/motion.js" defer></script>

    {/* The second script, and the only one that can pull in more. /globe.js paints
        nothing: it checks the window's width, whether there is a real pointer and
        whether there is a WebGL context, and fetches one of two fields on the answer.
        "Wide enough" is a mouse over 901px OR any screen at all over 1200px — the second
        clause is what gives a tablet the globe, and the file itself carries the measured
        reason the number is 1200.

        Yes — /three.min.js and /globe-scene.js: the globe in the sky behind the story on
        the front page, and on every other page the orb field that globe sits in, mounted
        from the same scene with the planet left out. That second case is why the fetch is
        no longer front-page-only: /globe.js used to stop when it found no `.curio` deck,
        and does not any more. One extra request on an interior page, cached across the
        site after the first.

        No — /sky-mobile.js: the same orb field, drawn in a 2D canvas, on every page. A
        PHONE STILL NEVER FETCHES /three.min.js, which is the point of splitting this
        file: the library is 589KB and a handset should not spend it on decoration. The
        20KB one is what it spends instead, and it arrived on 2026-09-22 because the field
        was a wide-screen-only thing and there was no reason for it to be. That holds
        even when a phone asks for the desktop site, which is the whole reason the second
        clause of the gate is 1200px and not 901.

        Both branches hide the front page's CSS sky — the nebula and the star tiles —
        from script rather than deleting it, so a reader with no JavaScript still gets
        that section the way it was built. The phone branch hid nothing for the first few
        hours of its life; on a 384px column that band of cloud belonged to one section
        and nothing else did, and the owner asked for the orbs alone.

        Named files at stable paths for the same reason /motion.js is one: the about
        page, README.md and src/site.ts all invite the reader to count the requests
        in their network panel, so every script has to be findable. Adding a sixth means
        editing those three in the same commit. */}
    <script is:inline src="/globe.js" defer></script>
  </head>
  <body>
    <header class="site-head">
      <div class="wrap wrap--bar">
       <div class="site-head__bar glass">
        {/* A mark and a wordmark, locked up.

            The mark is the supplied artwork: a cube with a torn-open face, an outlined
            lowercase m on the front and a second m revealed inside it. Double M, the long
            way round.

            It arrived as a 976x1098 RGB PNG on a WHITE background with no alpha, which on
            this ground would have been a white rectangle. The white is keyed out with a
            soft ramp — neutral pixels fade in proportion to their distance from white, so
            anti-aliased edges stay clean — and anything carrying real colour stays opaque.
            That is the teal linework, the mint m's, and the shaded inner face, which
            measured (204,213,222) at saturation 18 and so survives the key.

            A consequence worth knowing: the cube's own faces were white, so they key out
            too and the cube reads as an open wireframe here rather than as the solid white
            box it is on a light background. On this ground that is the better of the two.

            It is 40px, not the 13.5px the drawn monogram was. Rendered small it turns to
            mud — at 24px neither m is readable — so the size is set by the artwork, and
            tools/check-mark.mjs measures it. */}
        <a href="/" class="wordmark" aria-label="doubleem, home">
          <img class="wordmark__mark" src="/assets/mark-256.png" alt=""
               width="42" height="40" decoding="async" />
          <span class="wordmark__type">doubleem</span>
        </a>
        <nav aria-label="Main">
          {nav.map((item) => <a href={item.href}>{item.label}</a>)}
        </nav>
       </div>
      </div>
    </header>

    <main id="main">
      <slot />
    </main>

    <footer class="site-foot">
      <div class="wrap site-foot__inner">
        <p class="site-foot__mark">
          <img src="/assets/mark-256.png" alt="" width="50" height="48" decoding="async" loading="lazy" />
          <span>doubleem</span>
        </p>
        <p class="site-foot__line">
          One person, making things and putting them where people can find them.
          This site collects nothing about you.
        </p>
        <p class="site-foot__meta">&copy; {year} DOUBLEEM</p>
      </div>
    </footer>
  </body>
</html>

<style>
  /* ── The bar floats now, and the old note here was overtaken by events ──
     It used to be an opaque full-width strip, and the comment in this spot argued
     against a floating translucent pill on the grounds that it needs backdrop-filter to
     stay readable with a paragraph scrolling under it, "which this site dropped on
     purpose". That reasoning was sound at the time and is simply out of date: the design
     now has `--glass` and `--glass-blur` as first-class tokens, used by the feature
     panel and the bento, so the thing that made a floating bar unaffordable is standard
     equipment. Taking publik's floating bar costs nothing that is not already spent.

     What the float buys: content runs under the bar rather than stopping dead at a
     border, which is most of why publik's pages read as denser than this one did. The
     header is transparent and only the inner bar is a surface.

     The radius is the one place the small-radii rule bends, and it bends by the rule's
     own logic. "A 16px radius on everything is the look of a component library" is about
     putting a big curve on a small object — a 999px pill on a 12px label. This is a 58px
     bar; 18px on it is proportionally what --r-md is on a chip. It is not a licence to
     round anything else up. */
  .site-head {
    position: sticky;
    top: 0;
    z-index: 10;
    padding-block: var(--head-gap) 0;
    /* ── The bar used to be WIDER than the content, and it is not any more ──
       This is the second borrowed structure from publik to be handed back; DESIGN.md
       § The fourth change lists the others. publik insets its bar 30px from the window
       and its content 91px, so the nav spans almost the full width while the page sits
       in from it, and the bar reads as chrome belonging to the window rather than as
       the first row of the page. That is a real effect and it worked.

       The owner asked for it removed on 2026-09-21: on a wide window the bar ran
       34px to 1676px while everything beneath it ran 125px to 1585px, and the extra
       180px read as a bar that had been stretched rather than one that had been placed.
       The override that produced it — `max-width: none` plus a `--bar-inset` of
       clamp(12px, 2.1vw, 34px) — is gone, so .wrap--bar is now simply .wrap and the bar
       measures exactly what the page measures.

       The bar keeps its own 14px/12px padding, so the wordmark sits that far inside the
       headline's left edge rather than flush with it. That was offered and not taken:
       aligning the two would mean pulling the bar box back out past the measure, which
       is the thing being removed here. */
    /* No background and no border. A sticky element with neither still captures pointer
       events across its full width, which would swallow clicks on whatever scrolls
       under it — so the header itself is inert and only the bar inside it is not. */
    pointer-events: none;
  }
  .site-head__bar {
    pointer-events: auto;
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: 16px;
    min-height: 58px;
    padding-inline: 14px 12px;
    border-radius: 18px;
    box-shadow: 0 10px 34px -18px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.9);
  }
  /* The wordmark sits quietly beside the nav rather than announcing itself. Its full
     stop used to be accent-coloured — one of five accent things on the old homepage, and
     the least useful of them, since a decorative dot cannot be clicked. */
  .wordmark {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    /* Wider than the 6px the small monogram wanted: the mark is now a 42px picture with
       its own internal margins, and a lockup gap is measured from what you can see, not
       from the box. */
    gap: 11px;
    text-decoration: none;
    color: var(--ink);
  }
  .wordmark__mark {
    flex: none;
    width: 42px;
    height: auto;
    /* The artwork's own bounding box is its alignment: it was trimmed to its ink, so the
       cube's top and bottom edges are the image's top and bottom edges. No optical nudge
       — the drawn monogram needed one because it was cap height against a taller line
       box, and this is a picture that simply centres. */
    transition: transform 260ms var(--ease);
  }
  /* The wordmark is the one piece of text on the site NOT set in the display face. That
     is deliberate: Bricolage is the voice of the page's headings, and a wordmark that
     shares it reads as another heading rather than as a name. Albert Sans at 600 is
     already loaded — it is the text face — so this costs no extra bytes, which a third
     family for one word would not.

     Lowercase, and no full stop. Both of the rules that used to live here are therefore
     void and gone: the positive tracking existed because eight capitals have no
     lowercase to set their rhythm, and the negative right margin existed to pull back the
     space that tracking added after the full stop. Lowercase sets its own rhythm and
     wants the opposite — a touch tight. */
  .wordmark__type {
    font-family: var(--font);
    font-weight: 600;
    font-size: 25px;
    letter-spacing: -0.018em;
    line-height: 1;
  }

  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
    /* A small lift, nothing else. A monogram that rotates or bounces reads as a toy, and
       this one is eight capitals away from the word it abbreviates — it does not need to
       explain itself twice. */
    .wordmark:hover .wordmark__mark { transform: translateY(-2px); }
  }

  .site-head nav { display: flex; gap: 8px; }
  .site-head nav a {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    min-height: 44px;
    padding: 0 16px;
    border: 1px solid var(--hairline);
    border-radius: var(--r-lg);
    background: var(--surface);
    font-size: 14.5px;
    font-weight: 500;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    text-decoration: none;
    transition: border-color 150ms var(--ease), color 150ms var(--ease);
  }
  .site-head nav a:hover { color: var(--ink); border-color: var(--rule); }

  /* The wordmark is a link and measured 34px on a phone — the mark is 36px tall but the
     lockup's box was shorter. The nav chips get their 44 from the rule below, where the
     touch target and the drawn chip stopped being the same box. */
  @media (pointer: coarse) {
    .wordmark { min-height: 44px; }
  }

  /* The header is the one row on the site with four fixed-width things in it, and the
     mark going from 13.5px to 42px is what pushed it over: at 360px the page gained 4px
     of horizontal scroll on every route. Everything in the row gives a little rather than
     one thing giving a lot, because the mark cannot drop much below 36px before the two
     m's inside the cube stop resolving.

     The wordmark shrinks to 20px, not the 16px it held while it was capitals — lowercase
     of the same nominal size reads smaller, so it needed the opposite adjustment from
     what you would expect. 22px is the real ceiling: it fits at 360px and overruns at
     320px. Measured at 320/360/390/430/460. */
  @media (max-width: 460px) {
    .wordmark { gap: 8px; }
    .wordmark__mark { width: 36px; }
    .wordmark__type { font-size: 20px; }
    .site-head__bar { padding-inline: 10px 8px; min-height: 52px; }
    .site-head nav { gap: 6px; }

    /* ── The chip you see is 34px; the chip you can hit is still 44 ──
       These were 44px tall inside a 52px bar — 85% of its height, with 4px of air above
       and below — and the owner reported them on 2026-09-21 as looking too big. They
       were: at that ratio a chip stops reading as a chip in a bar and starts reading as
       a slab filling it. Measured against the alternatives on an SM-S928N:

           44px (as shipped)  0.85 of the bar   the reported problem
           36px               0.69              fine
           34px               0.65              chosen — sits with the 36px mark
           32px               0.62              text starts to crowd its own border

       34 is also what the wordmark beside it wants: the cube is 36px, so a 34px chip is
       the same object at the same scale rather than a taller one competing with it.

       ── Why the height moved and the touch target did not ──
       DESIGN.md § Accessibility floor sets 44x44 for touch targets and gives the reason:
       the audience is on phones. So the BORDER shrank and the TARGET did not. The ::after
       is an invisible overlay, centred on the chip and 44px tall whatever the chip is. It
       carries no paint, it sits inside the anchor so a press on it is a press on the
       link, and it cannot reach its neighbour because it grows only vertically while the
       6px gap between chips is horizontal.

       THE HEIGHT IS DECLARED, NOT DERIVED, and the first version of this got that wrong.
       It said `inset: -5px 0`, reasoning 34 + 5 + 5 = 44. It measured **42**: `inset` on
       an absolutely positioned box resolves against its containing block's PADDING box,
       and this anchor has a 1px border, so each -5px landed 4px outside the chip you can
       see. Writing `height: 44px` says the number the floor actually requires, and it
       stays right if the border or the chip height ever changes. Verified by walking
       elementFromPoint outward from each chip's centre until the anchor stops answering.

       Width needs no help: the narrower chip still measures 53px. */
    .site-head nav a {
      position: relative;
      min-height: 34px;
      padding: 0 10px;
      font-size: 13px;
    }
    .site-head nav a::after {
      content: '';
      position: absolute;
      left: 0;
      right: 0;
      top: 50%;
      height: 44px;
      transform: translateY(-50%);
    }
  }
  .site-head nav a[aria-current="page"] { color: var(--ink); border-color: var(--rule); }

  /* The footer is a raised panel rather than an inversion. It was the one dark block on
     a paper site; now that the whole site is dark, "inverted" means nothing and the only
     way to separate the footer from the page is to lift it — a lighter surface, a rule
     across the top, and the glow behind it still showing at the edges.

     Every rgba(255,255,255,…) that used to be in here is gone. Those values were written
     when the footer was guaranteed to be the darkest thing on the page; they now sit on
     --panel, which is the lightest, and white-at-45%-opacity on it measured 2.9:1. */
  /* ── Deliberately modest ──
     This was half again as tall: 64px of clearance, 52px of padding, a 50px mark and a
     29px wordmark, which gave the sign-off more presence than most of the sections above
     it. A footer that restates the site's name at nearly headline size competes with the
     page for no reason — it is a sign-off, not a section. */
  .site-foot {
    margin-top: 44px;
    background: var(--panel);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
    color: var(--ink);
  }
  .site-foot__inner { padding-block: clamp(26px, 3.2vw, 34px); }
  .site-foot__mark {
    margin: 0 0 10px;
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 10px;
  }
  .site-foot__mark img { flex: none; width: 32px; height: auto; }
  .site-foot__mark span {
    font-family: var(--font);
    font-weight: 600;
    font-size: 19px;
    letter-spacing: -0.018em;
    line-height: 1;
  }
  .site-foot__line {
    margin: 0 0 12px;
    max-width: 46ch;
    font-size: 14.5px;
    line-height: 1.55;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }
  .site-foot__meta {
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--font);
    font-size: 12.5px;
    letter-spacing: 0.005em;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    opacity: .8;
  }
</style>
